WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.960
SPEAKER 1: Mr. Churchill,
scene 2, take 1.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.440
WINSTON CHURCHILL:
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.500
permit me to claim
your attention.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.344
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:15.580
LARRY P. ARNN: If you're
around Hillsdale for very long,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:18.210
you'll find out that Winston
Churchill is important.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.280
Why?

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.520
Churchill did things
that no one's ever done.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.990
He was in the center of the
two greatest wars in history.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.460
Churchill was smart,
lightning quick.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.580
He was funny.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.390
WINSTON CHURCHILL: I
don't often do that.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.070
LARRY P. ARNN: Also,
he was eloquent.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.120
And eloquent in a greater
profusion than I think anybody.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.170
You know, we remember
things he said.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.545
WINSTON CHURCHILL:
We've must now

00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:50.505
give out all our
strength and resources

00:00:50.505 --> 00:00:56.210
to the completion of our
tasks, both at home and abroad.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.080
Advance Britannia.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.750
Long live the cause of freedom.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.552
God Save the King.

00:01:03.552 --> 00:01:05.760
LARRY P. ARNN: He won the
Nobel Prize for Literature.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.150
He wrote about 50 books.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.910
He worked very hard
and very long hours.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.270
Sometimes he'd have people with
him until 2:00 in the morning.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.740
And he organized
his life to produce

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.750
15 or 20 hours of work a day.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.430
And then he was
involved in everything.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.650
So his archive is huge.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.210
The story of his life is huge.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.330
And you know, he became, at the
end of his life, the greatest

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.860
man in the world.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.010
So that means that
everything in his life

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.960
becomes a subject of study.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.360
And Churchill
understood all that.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.260
And Churchill had
this very bright son.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.130
And Churchill said,
wrote to him and said,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.190
I want you to
write my biography.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:58.450
So in 1961 and -2, Randolph
Churchill launches this thing.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.850
He hired a research assistant
named Martin Gilbert.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.200
And Randolph Churchill
died in 1968.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.720
And Martin Gilbert,
a young historian

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.000
who'd been working
on the biography

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.330
and had published three
books already at a young age,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:16.410
got picked.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.450
And in 1977, I was
a Rotary fellow.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.640
I had a scholarship
to go to England.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.452
I was going to write my
doctoral thesis on Churchill.

00:02:24.452 --> 00:02:26.410
And then one of my teachers
knew Martin Gilbert

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.940
and asked him to meet me.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.430
And so we had a talk and,
well, he offered me a job.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.257
August, 1977.

00:02:36.257 --> 00:02:37.840
Two weeks later he
hired a woman who's

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.550
now my wife of almost 40 years.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.420
And then in 2001,
darned if he didn't up

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.020
and asked me for a job
here at Hillsdale College.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.200
And I gave it to him.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:01.530
Anyway, I said, I need
to buy your papers,

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.170
because we made a deal
about 20 years ago

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.120
that if you didn't
finish the document

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.240
volumes to the biography,
we made a deal that if you

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.630
didn't finish it, I would.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.848
Because Churchill set this up.

00:03:13.848 --> 00:03:15.890
WINSTON CHURCHILL: We
shall fight on the beaches.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:19.410
We shall fight on
the landing grounds.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:23.060
We shall fight in the
fields and in the streets.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.430
We shall fight in the hills.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.830
We shall never surrender.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.380
LARRY P. ARNN: The college
has the Churchill Project now,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.040
which have just been
finished, and now number 23.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.650
So 8 plus 23, there
are 31 volumes

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.800
in the official biography
of Winston Churchill.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.860
We want people to understand
Churchill, first of all,

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.620
because it's just beautiful to
see some of the things he did

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.310
and understand
what produced them.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:51.130
It tells you something
about the nature

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.620
of human life, as
Churchill would say,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.085
both its triumph
and its tragedy.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.670
But then second,
we want people who

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.700
are interested in
governments, being

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.580
leading citizens,
including politicians,

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.650
we want them to learn from this
guy who has so much to teach.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:15.190
Here's something that's
done that's worthy,

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:19.070
that's going to have
an abiding influence.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:22.440
And it's not going
to make anybody rich.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.670
And if you want to honor
the freedom in the things

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.640
and fight for it, people who
fight for it and secure it,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.540
and the truth, what
better way to do it?

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.670
We intend to tell the
story of Churchill

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.980
and make it
available in new ways

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.770
and keep the idea
of Churchill alive.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.890
Because Churchill is an
example of a kind of thing

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.500
that is essential
to human nature

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.460
and helps to solve some of the
essential problems it faces.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.750
In the moments when
things mattered a lot,

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.820
people often looked
to Churchill,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.960
including in the moment
where they mattered the most.

